# TICKET-116 — next-env.d.ts is generated, and with two build directories in play
# it CHURNS. Next rewrites its `/// <reference path="./<distDir>/types/routes.d.ts" />`
# line to whichever build ran most recently, so the file says `.next-e2e` after an
# e2e run and flips back to `.next` after `next dev`/`npm run build`. That is a
# permanently dirty tree and a merge conflict on every future branch. There is no
# config switch to stop it: Next REWRITES that line (unlike tsconfig.json's
# `include`, which it only APPENDS to — which is why tsconfig carries BOTH type
# directories above and is stable).
#
# Untracking is safe here, measured rather than assumed:
#   - it is pure generated output: ONE commit in this repo's entire history (the
#     initial scaffold), never hand-edited since;
#   - `next build` recreates it byte-identically when absent, and a full
#     `npm run build` (type check + es-target + css-target) is GREEN without it;
#   - `npx tsc --noEmit` reports the IDENTICAL error count with and without it
#     (2965 either way) — the route types reach the program through tsconfig's
#     `include` globs, not through this file's reference line.
# Residual cost: a fresh clone has no next-env.d.ts until the first `next`
# command generates one. This is a deliberate deviation from the Next.js default.
next-env.d.ts
